.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1149 PM PDT Wed Oct 14 2026
(Tonight through Friday)

Near critical fire weather conditions continue across the higher
elevations, particularly the North Bay Interior Mountains, through
Friday. A positively tilted upper level ridge remains over Oregon
and Northern/Central California while a cut-off low lingers over
Southern California. This setup promotes light to moderate offshore
winds across the region with the potential for more moderate to
gusty offshore winds across the higher elevations. Model guidance
keeps gusts across the elevated terrain peaking between 20 to 30 mph
with locally stronger gusts possible across the highest peaks (e.g.
Mt. St. Helena). The GFS and HRRR are both in agreement that the SFO-
WMC gradient will peak around -9 mb Thursday morning and -10 mb
Thursday night into Friday. The NAM continues to be an outlier and
suggests the gradient could peak around -14 mb but looking closer
into their forecast wind gusts still keeps gusts in a similar, but
slightly stronger range, of 25 to 35 mph. Very dry conditions are
expected across the higher elevations during both the daytime and
overnight hours. Much of the region will see daytime RH in the 10s
to 20s with poor to very poor overnight recoveries with the worst
recoveries across the higher elevations. Given the very dry
conditions and potential for offshore winds, any spark has the
potential to start a fire. Remember, one less spark one less
wildfire.

Warmer temperatures continue Thursday and Friday with highs in the
